<h2>The marriage and the litigation</h2>
<p>The parties married in December 2014 and had no children. According to the wife, the marriage was marked almost from the start by demands for dowry — a Fortuner car and Rs 25 lakh — accompanied by physical and mental cruelty, including an incident in 2015 in which she alleged an attempt to strangle her. The parties separated in mid-2015 and never resumed cohabitation. Two FIRs were registered on her complaints: FIR No. 1746/2015 under Sections 307 and 34 of the Indian Penal Code, and FIR No. 207/2016 under Sections 498A, 406, 323 and 34.</p>
<p>In March 2023 the wife petitioned for divorce under Section 13(1)(ia) and (ib) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 (HMA No. 761/2023). The Family Court granted the decree on 9 September 2024. The husband appealed to the Delhi High Court under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act, 1984 read with Section 28 of the Hindu Marriage Act, contending that the wife&rsquo;s allegations were uncorroborated and that he had been denied an equal opportunity to make final arguments.</p>

<h2>The decisive fact: an empty cross-examination</h2>
<p>What decided the appeal was not a dispute over the law of cruelty but the state of the trial record. The wife&rsquo;s deposition had gone unchallenged, because the husband&rsquo;s counsel put no suggestions to her in cross-examination — and, as the Bench noted, counsel told the Court that this was a deliberate tactical choice. Relying on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decisions in <em>Muddasani Venkata Narsaiah</em> and <em>Mahavir Singh v. State of Haryana</em>, the Division Bench held that a party cannot challenge in appeal facts he chose not to contest when the witness was before the court.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Witness deposes.</strong> The wife testified to dowry demands, violence and the 2015 strangling incident.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Cross-examination declined.</strong> No suggestions were put to her; counsel confirmed the choice was tactical.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Testimony stands.</strong> The unrebutted deposition became the factual foundation of the decree.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal fails.</strong> The appellate court will not reopen facts the appellant declined to contest at trial.</div>
</div>

<h2>Cruelty: a concept without a closed list</h2>
<p>On the substance, the Bench applied the settled understanding of matrimonial cruelty. It quoted <em>Ravi Kumar v. Julmidevi</em> — &ldquo;cruelty in matrimonial behaviour defies any definition and its categories can never be closed&rdquo; — and <em>Roopa Soni v. Kamalnarayan Soni</em>, which observes that the word &ldquo;cruelty&rdquo; in Section 13(1)(ia) has no fixed meaning. Against that standard, the wife&rsquo;s account was not a bare assertion: it was backed by contemporaneous police action and two FIRs. The husband&rsquo;s plea that he had been falsely implicated, by contrast, was unsupported by any evidence on the record.</p>

<h2>The natural-justice plea and the purpose of the appeal</h2>
<p>The husband&rsquo;s remaining argument was procedural: that he was denied a fair chance to argue the matter finally. The order sheets answered it. He and his counsel were present on all three dates fixed for final hearing. The Bench rejected the plea on the record itself.</p>
<p>There remained a candid admission that coloured the whole appeal. The husband told the Court that he did not object to the divorce as such; the appeal was pursued mainly to &ldquo;clear his image.&rdquo; The Bench recorded its displeasure at this use of appellate remedies. The appeal was dismissed and the decree of divorce affirmed, with no order as to costs.</p>

<h2>What practitioners and parties should take away</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Cross-examination is not optional theatre. In matrimonial trials, where the parties are usually the principal witnesses, the cross-examination of the opposing spouse is frequently the only instrument for displacing their account. A conscious decision to forgo it is, in substance, a concession on the facts.</p>
  <p>Suggestions matter. The formal purpose of putting one&rsquo;s own case to the witness is to give them an opportunity to deny it; the practical consequence of omitting it is that the appellate court treats the testimony as admitted.</p>
  <p>Procedural grievances must match the order sheets. Pleas of denied hearing are tested against the court&rsquo;s own record of appearances, not against recollection.</p>
  <p>An appeal is not a press release. Where a party accepts the decree but litigates for reputation, courts notice — and say so in the judgment, which rarely serves the reputation in question.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article describes a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice, and the outcome of any matrimonial appeal depends on the evidence and record of the individual case.</div>
